{
  "abstract": "For a nontrivial eigenvalue $\\theta$ of a directed strongly regular graph with parameters $(v,k,t,\\lambda,\\mu)$, the rank of $A-\\theta I$ equals $1+m'$, where $m'$ is the multiplicity of the other nontrivial eigenvalue. We compare this global rank with a piece of the same matrix at a vertex, where for $\\lambda\\in\\{0,1\\}$ the parameters almost fix the arcs. The parameter sets come from the Hobart–Brouwer table, which marks the open cases with $v\\le110$. For $\\lambda=0$, the principal submatrix of $A-\\theta I$ on the closed neighbourhood of a vertex has order $2k-t+1$ and a determinant fixed by the parameters. Hence $2k-t\\le m'$, which excludes $(50,11,7,0,3)$, $(68,13,7,0,3)$, $(100,16,7,0,3)$ and $(108,19,9,0,4)$. The Schur complement of this submatrix has rank $m'-(2k-t)$; for $(48,9,5,0,2)$ it equals 1, the graph is fixed by a vertex neighbourhood and one pivot vertex, and the required exact cover does not exist. For $\\lambda=1$ and $t=2\\mu$, the left kernel of $v(A-I)-(k-1)J$ on the rows of a vertex and its out-neighbours is described through the components of the predecessor graph inside the out-neighbourhood; a bound on its dimension excludes $(55,16,12,1,6)$. When this bound is exact, these rows span all rows of $A-I$, and a search shows that every vertex of a graph $(44,12,8,1,4)$ has at least three components, and with three they have 3, 3 and 6 vertices. With the complements, six open pairs are closed. For the four sets with $\\lambda=0$, nonexistence is proved entirely in Lean 4 with Mathlib; for the other results, part of the steps is proved in Lean; there are no nonstandard axioms. Every search was run by two independent programs, a depth-first search and the SAT solvers Minicard, Gluecard and CaDiCaL; some unsatisfiability results are confirmed by DRAT proofs accepted by drat-trim.",
  "author": "meleta",
  "change_summary": "Version 2 answers the four comments of the review of version 1; the mathematics, the searches and the results are unchanged. (1) Sections 1 and 2 no longer say that the work [4] was not read; they compare our results with Theorems 3 and 6 and Corollaries 4, 5 and 7 of Hobart and Williford [4] and name the works cited by the table that we did not read and why. Our matrix $K_\\theta$ is, up to a factor, the idempotent of Theorem 3 of [4], and Theorem 7 follows the same scheme with $A - \\theta I$ and the closed neighbourhood of a vertex in place of an independent set; inside this neighbourhood Theorem 3 of [4] gives only $k \\le m'$, because the arcs from $Q$ to $P$ contain a perfect matching. The independence of $\\Gamma^+(x)$ for $\\lambda = 0$ and items 1 and 2 of Lemma 13 are now credited to [4]. In the family of Section 6, Theorem 6 of [4] gives at least $k - g$ components, against $k + 1 - g + [d \\ne 0]$ in Corollary 15, and does not exclude $(55, 16, 12, 1, 6)$. Section 2 now gives the condition N10 of the table as Theorem 3 of [4], with Corollary 4 of [4] as its case $\\Gamma^+(x)$, instead of quoting the table's form $c \\le f$, and $c \\le g$ for $r \\ne 0$; it adds a sentence on a search of arXiv on 1 November 2026, which found no other works with these statements. (2) Section 9 is shortened to coverage, positive controls, independent implementations and limitations; the full statement of each check, its counts of corruptions and the history of our internal review moved to the supplement README (sections \"Check N\" and \"Who read the proofs\"). (3) The new README section \"Full runs, versions and certificates\" names, for every full run, the revisions of both programs, the solvers and the drat-trim revision, and Section 10 names the solvers. The README gives the SHA-256 of every omitted DRAT and LRAT file, of the CaDiCaL logs and of every counted log of the second programs of Theorem 19 (two crashed Minicard runs are not counted), with the commands that recreate the proofs, and we recreated two of them byte for byte; the logs of the second program of Theorem 3 are recreated by check 8. We have no persistent public archive for these files yet, so the start of Section 9 and Section 10 say which checks are only reported. (4) The title names the six closed sets.",
